sequenceDiagram
  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task, DECISIONS and R1-R4, agent-observer-swamp-acts, single-swamp-node. docs/reference/private-dns.md#resolver-selection.
  %% Tightened: flow-tighten 2026-11-02, rules 6, 20, 22 (persist and set the policy in one step; the read's match acts as a guard).
  %% status: draft
  %% command: none · routing domains change
  %% kind: fleet.peer_dns
  autonumber
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209
  participant N as Target Node<br/>reached over ssh
  Note over GW: Domains changed<br/>task #5163 · peer Node 12
  %%> When:
  %%> - the private routing domains or the selected DNS server change, for example a Node with a new TLD joins or the `vpn` role moves
  %%> - the Gateway admits one task per peer, one peer at a time
  %%> - the Node agent reports a different DNS policy on Node 12 than the Gateway wants
  %%> Not included:
  %%> - no command; no per-minute Swamp poll; drift comes from the Node agent's reports
  %%> - a full re-apply of Node 12's footprint is `node.converge`, which uses the same DNS steps
  %%> Requirements:
  %%> - Node 12 is an active managed Linux peer with roles; the `vpn` Node and the Gateway stay out
  %%> - no other network change runs on Node 12; the Gateway holds one lock per Node
  %%> Result:
  %%> - task #5163 recorded with the domains and the DNS server for Node 12
  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-fleet-peer-dns
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - starts one Swamp run on serve (Node 209)
  %%> Inputs:
  %%> - the routing domains, such as `~orbit`, and the DNS server's WireGuard address
  %%> - Node 12's address and SSH host keys; the SSH identity comes from the `swamp` role
  SW->>N: Read the DNS policy<br/>wg show · resolvectl
  %%> How:
  %%> - Swamp reaches the Node over SSH from 209, with `@swamp/ssh`
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - one read command prints the public key, resolver servers, `orbit` domains and default route, the DNS marker and the hook line
  Note over SW: Decide the DNS change<br/>typed check
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - compares the read with the admitted policy; a match skips the DNS writes below
  %%> - refuses a live key that is not the prepared one
  SW->>N: Keep the DNS hook<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt/line
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - keeps the Gateway-rendered `PostUp = resolvectl …` line in `orbit.conf`, so the policy returns when the tunnel starts
  %%> - leaves every other line of the file as it is
  SW->>N: Write the DNS marker<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt/file · 0600
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - records the link, server and domains in `/etc/wireguard/orbit.dns-link`
  SW->>N: Set the DNS policy<br/>resolvectl dns · domain · route
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - one command sets the server and routing domains on `orbit` with `default-route no`; only Orbit names go to VPN DNS
  opt policy was on another link
    SW->>N: Clear the old link<br/>dns · domains · networkctl
    %%> When:
    %%> - only when an earlier release put the policy on another link
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - one command clears the old server and domains there, gives the uplink its default route back and reconfigures it
  end
  SW->>N: Read the DNS policy again<br/>wg show · resolvectl
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - the same read as before, for the end check
  Note over SW: Check the DNS policy<br/>typed check
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - requires the admitted policy on `orbit` and a usable uplink
  alt both names resolve
    N-->>SW: private + public ok
    %%> Returns:
    %%> - the check passed
    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event
    %%> How the Gateway learns it:
    %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event; Swamp never calls the Gateway
    Note over GW: Record the DNS policy<br/>Node 12 · task succeeded
    %%> Result:
    %%> - Node 12's peer DNS digest is recorded; task #5163 succeeds
    %%> - the Gateway admits the next peer
  else setting or checking fails
    N--xSW: exit ≠ 0 or no answer
    %%> When:
    %%> - `resolvectl` refuses, SSH fails, or a name does not resolve
    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step
    %%> The Gateway reads:
    %%> - which step failed and its error code
    Note over GW: Mark the peer failed<br/>stop · rerun after a fix
    %%> Result:
    %%> - Node 12's peer DNS is marked failed; task #5163 fails; later peers wait
    %%> Retry:
    %%> - safe: the first step skips a policy that already matches; it is a new task
  end
